    "['local-address [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor peer-AS100 update-source 11.1.1.1\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco:\\n1. The BGP AS number (100) needs to be specified in the `router bgp` command\\n2. The `local-address` parameter in Juniper translates to `update-source` in Cisco\\n3. The neighbor address would need to be specified in a separate `neighbor x.x.x.x remote-as` command (not shown here as we were only asked to translate the local-address command)\\n4. The group name \"peer-AS100\" is used as the neighbor identifier in this translation, though in Cisco you would typically use the neighbor\\'s IP address directly', 'trans_template': 'router bgp 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor peer-AS100 update-source 11.1.1.1', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor peer-AS100 update-source 11.1.1.1\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco:\\n1. The BGP AS number (100) needs to be specified in the `router bgp` command\\n2. The `local-address` parameter in Juniper translates to `update-source` in Cisco\\n3. The neighbor address would need to be specified in a separate `neighbor x.x.x.x remote-as` command (not shown here as we were only asked to translate the local-address command)\\n4. The group name \"peer-AS100\" is used as the neighbor identifier in this translation, though in Cisco you would typically use the neighbor\\'s IP address directly', 'trans_template': 'neighbor peer-AS100 update-source 11.1.1.1'}]]": 1,
    "['neighbor [parameter2] peer-as [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the given Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 11.1.1.2 remote-as 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco, you first need to enter the BGP process with the local AS number (100 in this case, as seen from the peer-as 100 in the Juniper config), then configure the neighbor with its AS number. The Juniper command doesn't show the local AS number explicitly, but it's implied by the peer-as value in the neighbor configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'router bgp 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 11.1.1.2 remote-as 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the given Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 11.1.1.2 remote-as 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco, you first need to enter the BGP process with the local AS number (100 in this case, as seen from the peer-as 100 in the Juniper config), then configure the neighbor with its AS number. The Juniper command doesn't show the local AS number explicitly, but it's implied by the peer-as value in the neighbor configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'neighbor 11.1.1.2 remote-as 100'}]]": 1,
